reply='''Tested head 8997958d8e6419e384e68057fb37fe09a148e697 against base 8b4eae66da2938ba9559f103b18dbf85cdf28a70 in a disposable QEMU/KVM Omarchy desktop via `omarchy dev ui-preview dropdown` (the real shared-component gallery).

The complete acceptance run passed: https://github.com/quanru/doubao-say/actions/runs/38748420827 (harness 978b6fa9365ee8603103c45545dfcbb745c7692a). Independently reviewed screenshots show closed wheel selection advancing clock → weather → power and reversing, with both ends clamped; the baseline remains unchanged. The gallery's unmatched initial value "Clock" lands on omarchy.clock after one downward notch. Wheeling while the popup is open preserves the selected value and keeps it open; Escape, keyboard selection and clicking the existing selected option still work.

Horizontal left/right tests used a temporary Linux uinput mouse. Guest evdev records confirm REL_HWHEEL -1/+1 with no REL_WHEEL contamination, and selection stays unchanged on both base and head. QMP's named wheel-left was separately diagnosed as a vertical event in this configuration and was not used as horizontal proof.

Scope: Qt 6.11.2 / Quickshell 0.3.1, pinned packages from the official Arch archive. The current Qt 6.12 runtime exposed a separate Color resolution compatibility problem before this matched-runtime run. Fine-grained touchpad accumulation, the opposite unmatched-value direction, clicking a different option, and business-panel end-to-end behavior were not verified. Original component/gallery bytes, owners and modes were restored; temporary input device/socket were removed. No model calls were made. Evidence is supplied as an offline report bundle with original Midscene HTML; no evidence site has been published.'''
(dest/'english-pr-reply-draft.txt').write_text(reply)
